# Line-ending policy for artifacts consumed inside Linux containers.
#
# The repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so without a
# pin git writes CRLF into the working tree -- and the working tree *is* the
# Docker build context. A CRLF shell script fails to exec in the image
# (`#!/bin/sh`: no such file or directory), and CRLF line continuations break
# multi-line RUN instructions. Every one of these blobs is already LF in the
# index, so this pin renormalises nothing; it only stops the checkout from
# corrupting them.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Debian packaging (wave L4). Same hazard, sharper: dpkg-buildpackage execs
# debian/rules directly and runs the maintainer scripts, so a CRLF checkout
# yields `#!/bin/sh^M: not found` and the build or the install fails. The
# maintainer scripts carry no .sh suffix, so the *.sh pin above does not reach
# them. Blobs are already LF; this only stops a Windows checkout corrupting them.
debian/** text eol=lf

# Janitor pass 7 (K-H7, 2026-09-18): generated-doc rebase tax. With per-tool
# count pins gone (#243), docs/API.md, docs/_generated/**, and
# docs/vertical_engines/ENGINE_STATUS.md are the files every lane's rebase
# now collides on -- one lane's regeneration conflicts with the next lane's,
# on every merge, across every open PR, even though there is no "both sides"
# to reconcile: a generated file has no authorial content, only a
# to-be-regenerated derivative of the source tree.
#
# `merge=ours` resolves the conflict by keeping "our" (the rebase target's)
# copy and discarding "theirs" -- no conflict markers, ever. This is
# deliberately NOT "the correct merge": neither side is guaranteed correct
# after a rebase moves the underlying source tree, and the driver does not
# attempt to regenerate live (git's mid-rebase state cannot reliably support
# the generators' own `git show`/`git ls-tree --baseline HEAD` reads -- other
# files this generator depends on may be mid-conflict in the very same
# rebase). Regeneration remains a REQUIRED manual step after every rebase,
# exactly as before this driver existed (charter §4.1: commit code changes,
# THEN regenerate, THEN commit the regenerated file) -- this driver removes
# only the tax of hand-editing conflict markers in a file whose content is
# about to be thrown away and recomputed regardless. CI's existing --check
# gates (gen_engine_figures.py --check, test_generated_surface_doc_matches_
# the_generator, gen_api_docs.py --check, gen_portal_parity.py --check) are
# UNCHANGED and remain the actual correctness enforcement -- this driver
# only ever affects a lane's own local pre-push rebase, never what CI
# verifies before merge.
#
# One known, accepted side effect: a commit whose ENTIRE diff is a doc
# regeneration (no other changes) may be silently dropped by `git rebase`
# when this driver makes its diff a no-op against the new base. This is
# harmless given the mandatory post-rebase regeneration step above -- the
# doc gets regenerated fresh regardless of whether the old regen-only commit
# survived the rebase.
#
# Setup required once per clone (lives in .git/config, not tracked by this
# file -- a driver nobody installs is a no-op that looks like a fix):
#   git config merge.ours.driver true
docs/API.md                            merge=ours
docs/_generated/**                     merge=ours
docs/vertical_engines/ENGINE_STATUS.md merge=ours
